<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> heated gloves and mittens are worth it if your hands get cold even in good insulated gloves, or if you ski in very cold places. Choose a pair that's warm and waterproof without power, with several heat settings, batteries that fit comfortably at the cuff, and run time that covers your ski day on the settings you'll actually use. Heated mittens stay warmer than heated gloves.</p></div>

<h2>Who heated gloves are for</h2>
<p>Most skiers stay comfortable in a good pair of insulated, waterproof gloves or mittens. Heated gloves make the biggest difference for:</p>
<ul>
  <li>People whose hands get cold quickly, including those with poor circulation or conditions like Raynaud's.</li>
  <li>Skiers in very cold regions, or anyone riding long, windy chairlifts.</li>
  <li>People who stop often, such as parents skiing with kids, instructors, and photographers.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your hands are only cold now and then, start with warmer mittens, glove liners, and hand warmers. See <%= link_to "how to choose ski gloves", guide_path("best-ski-gloves") %> first.</p>

<h2>How heated gloves work</h2>
<p>Thin heating elements are built into the glove, usually across the back of the hand and along the fingers. A rechargeable battery, typically in a pocket at the cuff, powers them. A button lets you pick a heat level, often low, medium, and high.</p>

<h2>What to look for</h2>
<h3>Warm and waterproof without power</h3>
<p>This is the most important feature. Batteries run out, and you'll ski plenty of runs with the heat off or low. A heated glove should still be a good insulated, waterproof ski glove on its own, with a membrane, a durable shell, and a gauntlet or cuff that seals over your jacket.</p>
<h3>Where the heat goes</h3>
<p>Fingertips get cold first. Check whether the heating elements run along the fingers and over the fingertips, not just the back of the hand.</p>
<h3>Heat settings and battery life</h3>
<p>Battery life depends heavily on the heat setting and the temperature outside. High heat drains batteries fastest. Look at the run time the maker lists for each setting, and plan on using low or medium for most of the day and high for cold lift rides. A spare battery pair can cover a long day.</p>
<h3>Battery size and placement</h3>
<p>Batteries add weight and bulk at the cuff. Try the gloves with batteries installed and make sure they fit under or over your jacket sleeve comfortably.</p>
<h3>Charging</h3>
<p>Check how batteries charge (a dedicated charger or a USB cable) and how long a full charge takes. Charging overnight is the norm. If you travel, confirm you can charge them where you're staying, and follow your airline's rules for lithium batteries in carry-on luggage.</p>
<h3>Fit and dexterity</h3>
<p>Heated gloves are bulkier than regular gloves. Make sure you can still work zippers, buckles, and your phone, and that the fingertips aren't so tight they restrict circulation.</p>

<h2>Heated gloves vs heated mittens</h2>
<p>Mittens keep your fingers together, so they share warmth and stay warmer than gloves, heated or not. Gloves give more dexterity for buckles, poles, and phones. If cold hands are your main problem, heated mittens are usually the better choice. Read <%= link_to "ski gloves vs mittens", guide_path("ski-gloves-vs-mittens") %> for the full comparison.</p>

<h2>Alternatives to try first</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Warmer mittens</strong> with a liner glove inside.</li>
  <li><strong>Disposable or rechargeable hand warmers</strong> tucked into a glove pocket.</li>
  <li><strong>Keeping your core warm:</strong> your body sends less blood to your hands when your core is cold, so a better mid layer can mean warmer fingers.</li>
  <li><strong>Drying gloves overnight:</strong> damp insulation feels cold.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Caring for heated gloves</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Remove the batteries before drying gloves, and never put them on high heat or a hot radiator.</li>
  <li>Charge batteries fully before storage, and store them in a cool, dry place over summer.</li>
  <li>Follow the maker's washing instructions; most heated gloves shouldn't go in a washing machine.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Shop heated gloves</h2>
<p>Browse <%= gear_link "heated ski gloves", "heated ski gloves" %>, <%= gear_link "heated mittens", "heated mittens" %>, and <%= gear_link "hand warmers", "hand warmers" %>. For the rest of your layers, see <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</p>

<p>Cold feet too? See <%= link_to "the best heated socks for skiing", guide_path("best-heated-socks") %>, or compare <%= link_to "the best hand warmers", guide_path("best-hand-warmers") %>.</p>

